Q: pass data through GET parameters without array A: <p>Try this -</p>
<pre><code>

refresh() {
  this.$axios.get(&quot;/Operacional/GetRelatorio&quot;, {
    params: { 
      id: this.situacao.ID,
      descricao: this.situacao.Descricao
    }
  }).then(res =&gt; { 
     this.prazos = res.data;
     this.$refs.chart1.updateSeries([{
         name: 'NO PRAZO',
         data: [this.prazos.noPrazo, this.prazos.emDia, this.prazos.atrasadas]
      }]);
   });
}


OR

refresh() {
  // please Ensure the array contains data before sending
  if (!this.situacao || this.situacao.length === 0) return;

  this.$axios.get(&quot;/Operacional/GetRelatorio&quot;, {
    params: { 
      id: this.situacao[0].ID,
      descricao: this.situacao[0].Descricao
    }
  }).then(res =&gt; { 
     this.prazos = res.data;
     this.$refs.chart1.updateSeries([{
         name: 'NO PRAZO',
         data: [this.prazos.noPrazo, this.prazos.emDia, this.prazos.atrasadas]
      }]);
   });
}

</code></pre>

Q: Converting a Java Map object to a Properties object A: <p>I got the idea from <a href="https://www.baeldung.com/java-convert-properties-to-hashmap" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this article</a> but I modified to convert from a Map to Properties. This is needed if you may need to manipulate the inputs.</p>
<pre><code>Map&lt;String,String&gt; input = System.getenv();
Properties output = input.entrySet().stream().collect(
    Collectors.toMap(
        e -&gt; String.valueOf(e.getKey()),
        e -&gt; String.valueOf(e.getValue()),
        (prev, next) -&gt; next, Properties::new
    )
);
</code></pre>
<p>Also if you want to filter values from the Map.</p>
<pre><code>Map&lt;String,String&gt; input = System.getenv();
Properties output = input.entrySet()
.stream().filter(e -&gt; e.getKey().startsWith(&quot;XYZ&quot;)).collect(
    Collectors.toMap(
        e -&gt; String.valueOf(e.getKey()),
        e -&gt; String.valueOf(e.getValue()),
        (prev, next) -&gt; next, Properties::new
    )
);
</code></pre>

Q: Close dropdown menu when clicking outside of it A: <p>@Nikhil's answer got me further but has it's downsides. I came up with the following solution:</p>

<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>    $(document).click(function (e) {
        var target = $(e.target);
        // check the actual element being clicked is not the dropdown trigger itself
        if (!target.hasClass('dropdown-trigger') &amp;&amp; !target.closest('.dropdown-trigger').length) {
            // use the framework to close the dropdown instead of just hiding it: hiding it will require you to click the trigger 2 times to reopen!
            $('dropdown-trigger').dropdown('close');
        }
    });
</code></pre>

Q: How to connect to a OPC-UA server from PHP? A: <p><strong>Update 2026: there is a native PHP implementation.</strong></p>
<p>This project was recently born which deals with bringing communication to industrial machinery via native PHP, without any workarounds.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/php-opcua" rel="nofollow noreferrer">github.com/php-opcua</a></p>
<p>technically it is simple to use</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>use PhpOpcua\Client\ClientBuilder;

$client = ClientBuilder::create()
    -&gt;connect('opc.tcp://localhost:4840');

// Read server status
$status = $client-&gt;read('i=2259');
echo $status-&gt;getValue(); // 0 = Running

$client-&gt;disconnect();
</code></pre>
<p>If you look at the organization you will find various sub-repositories derived to handle different environments including a way to make opcua sessions persistent cross requests in php</p>
